---
import { icon } from "../lib/format.ts";
const submit = "https://github.com/devstreamhq/devstream/issues/new?template=submit-repo.yml";
---

<section class="explain" aria-label="How DevStream works">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div>
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Someone submits a repo</h3>
          <p>One GitHub issue: the repo, a category and a line on what it does. Anyone can do it, including the people who built it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>A bot checks it</h3>
          <p>Open source licence, a README with substance, a push in the last 90 days, at least 30 days old. Pass, and it's listed. No waiting on a person.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Developers Like it</h3>
          <p>Every repo gets its own Discussion. A thumbs-up there is a Like, and Likes set the order you see above.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
      <details>
        <summary>What counts as a Like?<Fragment set:html={icon("chevron-down")} /></summary>
        <p>
          A thumbs-up on the repo's Discussion, from an account that's at least 30 days old and has a public repo or a contribution in the
          last year. A batch of brand-new accounts can react all they like. The ranking won't move.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why not just sort by stars?<Fragment set:html={icon("chevron-down")} /></summary>
        <p>
          Stars pile up for years and almost never come down, so the same giants sit on top forever. A Like is a developer vouching for a
          repo now, and the weekly arrows show who's climbing.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where did a repo go?<Fragment set:html={icon("chevron-down")} /></summary>
        <p>
          A repo that goes a year without a push, gets archived or says it's unmaintained gets a 30-day warning, then moves to the <a
            href="/archived">archive</a>. It comes back by itself if things pick up. Deleted repos, and ones that lose their licence or README,
          get a week's notice, then come off.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cta" aria-labelledby="cta-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div>
      <h2 id="cta-h">Know a repo that deserves a spot?</h2>
      <p>Submitting takes a minute. The bot does the rest, and you'll hear back on the issue.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="btn btn-green" href={submit}><Fragment set:html={icon("plus")} />Submit a repo</a>
  </div>
</section>
